    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • The Volkswagen Group's Affordable BEV Quartet Has Almost Reached 150,000 Orders\nWhite C8 Chevy Corvette ZR1 Drags Blue Ford Mustang Shelby GT500, Someone Should Have Stayed Home\n13 photos\nPhoto: Wheels / YouTube\nThe videographer behind the ' Wheels ' channel on YouTube recently took a detour around BMP – aka Bradenton Motorsports Park in Florida for some cool excerpts from the FL2K competition, then headed back to one of its favorite venues. Well, now they’re back at BMP, most likely because they remembered they had this feisty encounter lying around the editing room…\nThis videographer is very passionate about a couple of racing tracks in North America: one is the iconic The Strip at Las Vegas Motor Speedway , which is among the very few in the country with a four-lane arrangement. The other one is MRP – aka Mission Raceway Park or Thunder by the River – which is an auto racing facility located in Mission, British Columbia, Canada, with an NHRA-sanctioned quarter-mile dragstrip.\nBut just recently the videographer decided to add a third track on its repertoire, the legendary Bradenton Motorsports Park in Florida , as they showed us excerpts from the cool FL2K competition, an event which gathered “the southeast’s fastest street cars.” We have seen quite a bunch of interesting skirmishes, and we’re glad the videographer also remembered about this one.\nThat’s because it’s a classic General Motors versus the Ford Motor Company encounter, albeit slightly novel in its format because the traditional S550 Ford Mustang Shelby GT500 muscle car, equipped with the 5.2-liter supercharged Predator V8, got into a battle with the modern C8 Chevrolet Corvette in one of its top configurations, the 1,064-horsepower ZR1 packing the brand-new 5.5-liter twin-turbo V8 behind the seats and a mid-engine configuration.\nNormally, the Shelby GT500 is a mid-ten to low-elevens car, depending on the in stock condition, depending on the surface, tires, and testing conditions; while GM says the latest ZR1 can do better than that, somewhere in the mid- to high nines depending on the track, tires, and weather. But, of course, either the blue Shelby GT500 or the white C8 ZR1 or both could be modified.\nWell, it’s safe to say that the human factor is also very important, as the Corvette driver launched better than its adversary and had a car-length advantage while passing the Christmas tree, but something happened afterward and the pursuing Shelby GT500 made a spectacular comeback to catch up and defeat its adversary with a 9.34s pass at 137 mph, while the ZR1 only achieved a 10.04s pass at a much lower trap speed of less than 125 mph.\nIf anyone wonders what happened with the one driver that should have stayed home instead of getting ashamed, the comments section, as always, has a ton of opinions about who’s best and what’s better – GM or Ford’s ride. So, what do you think happened there – did the C8 driver get cold feet when achieving the high speed, or got scared by the roaring Shelby coming back to win the skirmish?\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nC8 Chevy Corvette ZR1 Ford Mustang Shelby GT500 Chevrolet Ford GM vs Ford Bradenton Motorsports Park drag race wheels\nAbout the author: Aurel Niculescu\nAurel has aimed high all his life (literally, at 16 he was flying gliders all by himself) so in 2006 he switched careers and got hired as a writer at his favorite magazine. Since then, his work has been published both by print and online outlets, most recently right here, on autoevolution. Full profile\nUP NEXT 1969 GTO \"Judge\" Rocks 428 V8 and 4-Speed, Owner Let It Go After 30 Years Because Wife Said So",
